WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.069
Film Courage: So did you submit your screenplays
for ALL I WANT and SOMEONE THAT I USED TO

00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:07.590
KNOW to competitions (screenplay competitions)?

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.620
West Liang: No.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.639
No.

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:21.680
I have submitted previous scripts of mine
to competitions, either plays or screenplays.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.070
And I have’t won anything.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:29.430
I’ve been placed in various things but I
haven’t won anything.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.320
This goes back to what we talked about earlier
about taste.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:44.670
I do think that the market doesn’t always
know and I think for this film (ALL I WANT)

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.999
the intention was to always go out there and
shoot it.

00:00:47.999 --> 00:00:54.760
A great example is when we started to structure
the story and when we started to think about

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:05.770
how to bring that idea from our head to the
page to set and onto screen, I told (writer/actress)

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:15.430
Melissa Center that this script is probably
at max 75 pages and she was a little bit surprised

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:22.440
because I think the industry standard is probably
at least 90-95, 98 whatever.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:34.941
But having done my previous indie feature
where that script was clocked in at 98 or

00:01:34.941 --> 00:01:40.450
102 pages long and it was an indie film.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.040
We didn’t shoot on 2% of the script.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:50.250
And so we really had to go into post-production
in that film, really struggling to piece the

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.850
story together in a way that is coherent.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:58.570
So I knew that this film would be a really
short shooting schedule because of budget

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:05.170
because of schedule and so the intention was
how do we go into post-production in the editing

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.489
room with as much as the script as possible.

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:17.299
So that we can really go in there with all
the tools for the editor.

00:02:17.299 --> 00:02:29.769
It’s a waste of time and a waste of vision
to have a 98-page script and you go into a

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:32.909
10-day shoot schedule where you can only shoot
7-pages a day.

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:38.739
The math is basically going to squeeze out
15-20 pages out of your script.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:42.930
And so that’s a good example is that we
didn’t really write the script with the

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:48.510
intention of going out there and submitting
it to contests or going out there to try to

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:56.400
raise money, this was really the blue print
for this journey that we were going to go

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.400
on.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.780
It was a road map for this trip that we were
going to take.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:11.749
And so we nailed it down to a quote..unquote
short feature-length script by industry standard.

00:03:11.749 --> 00:03:17.300
But the end process is that we have a full-length,
feature film that is over an hour and a half.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:22.080
Film Courage: So you thought if I take out
20 pages that will save us X amount of dollars…I

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:28.189
don’t know how you figure out it…we won’t
be here two more days or three more days,

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:29.189
whatever.

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:30.189
West Liang: Right.

00:03:30.189 --> 00:03:31.189
Right.

00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:35.900
Because what I think it comes down to for
indie filmmaking is at some point it comes

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.719
down to math.

00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:47.439
If you are shooting 10 days and you are probably
going to be budgeted at 7, 8 pages a day,

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:48.439
right?

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:53.750
Then that means you are probably going to
have a 70, 80 page script and if you want

00:03:53.750 --> 00:04:00.569
to have enough time for the actors to play
around a little bit, if you want to give enough

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:07.790
time for your crew to give you a good set
in terms of lighting, if you want to have

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:17.280
a little room for making those mistakes on
set, then if you go into a production telling

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.940
your first AD that I need to shoot 10 pages
a day, that’s just really kinda of unfair

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.660
for the production.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:34.879
And what ends up happening is you go into
post-production with 20% of your script missing

00:04:34.879 --> 00:04:37.389
and it’s really tough.

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:42.229
Film Courage: So if you hadn’t made SOMEONE
I USED TO KNOW (and I’m sorry I don’t

00:04:42.229 --> 00:04:46.680
know how much time went by between both projects)
but you probably would have gone in with the

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.830
mindset of let’s do industry standard, let’s
do 98-pages.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.870
West Liang: Absolutely.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:05:00.419
You go in there and it’s purely artistic,
you go in there and express something.

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:02.400
And everybody is saying “Let’s do it.”

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.300
The director, producer and you do it.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.360
Then the first day of shooting, the crew and
the cast are getting to know each other and

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:18.160
then you miss one scene, even if it’s one
scene and then you go into the next day already

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.490
behind and then on the next day we’re behind
and the next thing you know at the end of

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:29.449
the production schedule, you’ve missed a
nice chunk of your movie.

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:38.160
So I think that without this experience, without
having gone through that, I probably would

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.030
have been like “Yeah, let’s go out there
with a full-length script and let’s just

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:49.800
do our best” and I think we would have probably
had some struggles in terms of narrative in

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:50.800
post-production.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.740
Film Courage: Sure and then there is the whole
romantic notion of “Hey!

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:57.400
I’m in LA and I’m on a film set.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.180
And this is cool and I’m [finally] doing
this!”

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.259
But then time and money slip away and reality
hits.

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:04.580
Have you always been so disciplined?

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:08.270
What that a trait which was gained from this
last production?

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:11.810
Or were there other things that kind of helped
you become this “on point.”

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.980
Because I’m sure that’s not fun to have
to be “that guy” on set but you essentially

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:17.050
have to.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:31.099
West Liang: I think it’s having an idea
of what you can and can’t accomplish.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:37.710
I was saying to some of our people on the
crew during pre-production was at some point

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:45.720
the artistic kind of journey becomes kind
of a business journey.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:54.450
And I literally was using words like ‘We
really have to manage assets and liabilities.’

00:06:54.450 --> 00:07:01.400
And a good example is you go into production
with a hundred page script and that’s your

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:02.400
asset.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.509
And then you look at the budget and you look
at your schedule and then suddenly that script

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:11.690
becomes a liability because you can’t shoot
the extra 15 pages.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.710
And the crew is like “Oh my gosh, we’re
behind!

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:15.710
Behind…Behind.”

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.710
And that puts a certain type of energy on
the set.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:30.419
So to answer your question, I think the consensus
between Melissa and I was always let’s prepare

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:35.009
this product for success.

00:07:35.009 --> 00:07:38.479
Let’s try to create a roadmap.

00:07:38.479 --> 00:07:43.520
And we wrote the movie for a location that
we knew that we had access to.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:49.189
And the location changed several times during
pre-production and I had to go back in there

00:07:49.189 --> 00:07:57.131
and basically tinker a little bit and so that’s
another thing is I think we have to go in

00:07:57.131 --> 00:08:02.530
there with as much information as you can.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.900
Film Courage: And I’m seeing still again,
seeking balance, one scale is tipping more

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.509
and then you’re rewriting to fit the scene.

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:13.080
It sound like you did an excellent job of
managing this.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.080
West Liang: Yes.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.300
We had an amazing cinematographer Ruben O’Malley.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.419
He came from New York.

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:24.719
I mean I literally met him…we had phone
conversations and emails leading into the

00:08:24.719 --> 00:08:29.249
production but I literally met him two days
before filming.

00:08:29.249 --> 00:08:33.550
I picked him up at Los Angeles International
Airport (LAX) with Melissa on Thursday.

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:39.279
We started shooting on Saturday and we did
a tech scout on Friday and we had dinner and

00:08:39.279 --> 00:08:41.789
talked about what we wanted to do.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:48.810
And we had an amazing editor Derek Drouin,
an amazing sound designer and composer, all

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:49.810
along the way.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:55.970
We had so many people who were getting paid
far less than they deserved.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:09:01.519
And I certainly was seeking a balance of trying
to make sure that all the pieces were in the

00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:07.600
right places but at the same time, it comes
down to the kind of group of people that you

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:08.429
have to collaborate with.

